window.__labLoaded({"id":"5.2","title":"The t-Distribution and Intervals for a Mean","goal":"Build a t-interval for a mean from nine nitrate readings, and see how the t critical value compares with z for small and larger samples.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"The sample","text":"The lesson's nine nitrate readings (mg/L). Because sigma is unknown we use the sample SD s, and the standard error is s / sqrt(n).","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nnitrate = np.array([3.1, 4.2, 2.8, 3.9, 4.5, 3.3, 4.1, 3.7, 3.5])\nn = len(nitrate)\nmean = nitrate.mean()\nse = nitrate.std(ddof=1) / np.sqrt(n)\n\nprint(f'mean = {mean:.3f}, s = {nitrate.std(ddof=1):.3f}, SE = {se:.3f}')","out":"mean = 3.678, s = 0.554, SE = 0.185","figs":[]},{"title":"The t-interval in one line","text":"stats.t.interval takes the confidence level, the degrees of freedom (n minus 1), the centre and the standard error, and returns the lower and upper limits.","code":"low, high = stats.t.interval(0.95, df=n - 1, loc=mean, scale=se)\n\nprint(f'df = {n - 1}')\nprint(f'95% CI (t) = ({low:.2f}, {high:.2f}) mg/L')","out":"df = 8\n95% CI (t) = (3.25, 4.10) mg/L","figs":[]},{"title":"Check it by hand, and compare with z","text":"The interval is mean plus or minus t* times SE. Using z* = 1.96 by mistake gives a narrower interval that claims too much certainty.","code":"t_star = stats.t.ppf(0.975, df=n - 1)\nz_star = stats.norm.ppf(0.975)\n\nprint(f't* = {t_star:.3f}, margin = {t_star * se:.3f}')\nprint(f'by hand: ({mean - t_star * se:.2f}, {mean + t_star * se:.2f})')\nprint(f'with z*: ({mean - z_star * se:.2f}, {mean + z_star * se:.2f})  too narrow')","out":"t* = 2.306, margin = 0.426\nby hand: (3.25, 4.10)\nwith z*: (3.32, 4.04)  too narrow","figs":[]},{"title":"t versus z for different sample sizes","text":"The t critical value is larger for small samples, because s is a shaky estimate of sigma. As n grows, t* shrinks towards z* = 1.960.","code":"print('  n   df   t*      z*')\nfor k in (5, 10, 30):\n    print(f'{k:3d}  {k - 1:3d}  {stats.t.ppf(0.975, k - 1):.3f}  {stats.norm.ppf(0.975):.3f}')","out":"  n   df   t*      z*\n  5    4  2.776  1.960\n 10    9  2.262  1.960\n 30   29  2.045  1.960","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"nitrate <- c(3.1, 4.2, 2.8, 3.9, 4.5, 3.3, 4.1, 3.7, 3.5)\nt.test(nitrate)$conf.int\nqt(0.975, df = 8)\nqt(0.975, df = c(4, 9, 29))\nqnorm(0.975)","expect":"conf.int 3.252 to 4.104; qt df = 8: 2.306; qt for df 4, 9, 29: 2.776 2.262 2.045; qnorm 1.960","note":"t.test(x) tests against 0 by default, but we only use its conf.int, which is the 95% t-interval for the mean."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Explore (Statistics button: Descriptives, 95% confidence interval for the mean); critical values from Transform > Compute Variable with IDF.T","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / nitrate.\nBEGIN DATA\n3.1 4.2 2.8 3.9 4.5 3.3 4.1 3.7 3.5\nEND DATA.\nEXAMINE VARIABLES=nitrate\n  /PLOT=NONE\n  /STATISTICS=DESCRIPTIVES\n  /CINTERVAL 95.\nCOMPUTE t5 = IDF.T(0.975, 4).\nCOMPUTE t10 = IDF.T(0.975, 9).\nCOMPUTE t30 = IDF.T(0.975, 29).\nCOMPUTE z = IDF.NORMAL(0.975, 0, 1).\nLIST VARIABLES=t5 t10 t30 z /CASES=FROM 1 TO 1.","note":"Explore's Descriptives table shows the 95% Confidence Interval for Mean (3.25 to 4.10). IDF.T(0.975, df) gives the t critical value."},"stata":{"code":"clear\ninput nitrate\n3.1\n4.2\n2.8\n3.9\n4.5\n3.3\n4.1\n3.7\n3.5\nend\nci means nitrate\ndisplay invttail(4, 0.025) \" \" invttail(9, 0.025) \" \" invttail(29, 0.025) \" \" invnormal(0.975)","note":"ci means gives the 95% t-interval. invttail(df, 0.025) is the upper-tail t critical value, which equals Python's stats.t.ppf(0.975, df)."},"try":["Change the level to 0.99 in stats.t.interval and predict whether the interval gets wider or narrower.","Add k = 100 and k = 1000 to the loop and watch t* approach 1.960.","Add one extreme reading of 9.0 to the data. What happens to the width of the interval?"]});
